@ Index:   Chess against the C47: legal moves, check, mate, draws, a 2-ply search, a book, a clock
@ Author:  Victor Valdes
@ Version: 0.4.0
@ Source:  https://github.com/valdesvj/chess47
@ Tested:  C47 simulator only (python/c47sim.py): tests/test_chess47.py, the moves, the scores and the choices of
@ Tested:  the search against python/chess47ref.py (the same algorithm), whose move generator gives the published
@ Tested:  perft counts; 10 matches against a python-chess player (tools/match47.py); not yet run on a calculator
@ Input:   none: XEQ 'CHESS47', then the keys
@ Output:  the board; at 0 your stack and LASTx as before
@
@ ---- What it is ----
@ Chess 47 lets you play chess against your calculator. The board is drawn on the screen and you move the pieces
@ with the number keys. The C47 knows all the rules (castling, en passant, promotion, check, checkmate, stalemate,
@ draw by repetition or by too little material), opens from a small book of known openings and then thinks two
@ moves ahead. It can also play against itself.
@
@ ---- How to use it ----
@ Load CHESS47 and press XEQ CHESS47. Setting up takes about 10 seconds. You have the white pieces and it is your move.
@   8 2 4 6   move the cursor up, down, left and right (as you see the board)
@   5         pick up the piece under the cursor (it shows white on black); move the cursor to the square where it
@             goes and press 5 again. 5 on the same square puts it back. An illegal move is refused.
@   +         swap sides: the C47 takes the side to move and you take the other. Press it at the start to play
@             black; the board turns so that black is at the bottom.
@   9         C47 vs C47: the calculator plays both sides until checkmate or a draw. Press any key to stop it and
@             take over the side to move.
@   3         two players: two people play on the calculator; the C47 only checks that every move is legal and
@             calls check, checkmate and the draws (+ or 9 give it a side again)
@   7         a new game of normal chess
@   ÷         a new Chess960 game (Fischer random chess): type the position's number with three digits (066 for
@             66; 518 is the normal start) or press ÷ again for a random one. To castle, pick the king and then its
@             own rook: the king goes to g or c and the rook to f or d. The opening book is not used.
@   1         the level: 2 (two moves ahead, about 7-12 s a move), 1 (one move ahead, a few seconds), 0 CRAZY
@             (random legal moves, with a crazy face by the title)
@   0         the end. A game that is not over is kept: the next XEQ CHESS47 goes on where you left it.
@ The right side shows your side and level, whose move it is (CHECK, CHECKMATE ... when it happens) and the last
@ seven moves, in standard algebraic notation as in a book (e4, Nf3, exd5, Nbd2, O-O, e8=Q, Qh7+, Ra8#). The clock at the top left shows each side's time left: 5 minutes each and 10 seconds more for every
@ move played. Only a person's clock runs (the C47's stops while it thinks, so it never loses on time). A person
@ whose time runs out loses (WHITE / BLACK LOST ON TIME), unless the opponent could not mate by any series of legal
@ moves (a bare king, for example): then it is a draw (DRAW: TIME, NO MATE).
@ Promotion is always to a queen. There is no 50-move rule.
@
@ ---- What it changes on your calculator ----
@ Your stack and LASTx are given back at the end. Registers R00-R19 are not touched; R20-R99 are used and cleared
@ at the end. While it runs it makes the matrices BD, GL, GH, OB and the variables TK, TW, TB (the time left), NW, NB; at the end
@ it deletes them, except the saved game: the variable CHN with GH, TW and TB (they stay until the game is over or
@ you start a new one). It works with short integers in UNSIGN mode and gives you back your own integer mode, then
@ sets WSIZE 64, GRMOD 0 and GRFNT 20.
@
@ ---- Programs and labels ----
@ One program, CHESS47, with one global label (CHESS47). Every routine inside is a local label (LBL 01-99), so
@ nothing else is added to your program catalogue. It uses the STRUCT commands (IF / ELSE / ENDIF, DO / WHILE /
@ ENDDO) of the C47 / R47 firmware, ATEXT and GRFNT, AGRAPH and the bitwise commands AND OR XOR NOT BS?.
@
@ ---- Technical notes (from the commented source) ----
@ CHESS47 v0.4.0 - Chess 47: a chess engine for the C47 / R47 (and the DM42n with the C47 firmware)
@ You play the white pieces against the C47 (+ hands your side to the C47: it plays the side to move).
@ The rules: every legal move, castling (not out of, through or into check), en passant, promotion (always to a
@ queen); check, checkmate and stalemate; draws by repetition and by material (no 50-move rule).
@ The engine (a port of the matrix "mini engine" idea of Micro-Max, H.G. Muller): the board is the 10 x 12 mailbox
@ of the row 1 of the matrix BD (column s = 10 row + col + 1: a1 22 ... h1 29 ... a8 92 ... h8 99, the border 8); a move
@ is from x 1000 + to; the score SC (material + piece-square, white positive) is kept up to date by each move from
@ the table PT (row piece + 11, column the square: the signed value of that piece there); the moves of a ply go
@ in a row ML, the undo data in a row UN. Board, ML, PT and UN are the rows of ONE matrix BD (23 x 120: row 1 the
@ board, 2-4 the move lists of the root, the replies, the moves played; 5-17 PT; 18-20 UN; 21 the knight masks,
@ 22 the square bits, 23 the masks of the castling rights; row 11, the empty piece's, columns 1-7: the killer move,
@ its piece and target, the king's and the castling rooks' first squares, the Chess960 number + 1): one INDEX, no
@ switching. The search: 2 plies (level 2) or 1, alpha-beta cuts, the captures first, the killer move (the reply
@ that refuted the last root move) tried first; python/chess47ref.py is the same algorithm in Python (the tests
@ compare the two; python/chess960.py for Chess960).
@ The 64-bit bitwise commands: the castling rights are a 4-bit word (1 white short, 2 white long, 4 black short,
@ 8 black long: AND clears a right, BS? tests one); the dark squares are worked out with AND, NOT and OR from the
@ glyph words (dither AND NOT mask OR glyph), WSIZE 24 while drawing: a square is 24 rows, one AGRAPH word a column;
@ the knights are two 64-bit words NW NB (WSIZE 64 the rest of the time): the attack test is one AND with a mask.
@ Keys: 8 2 4 6 the cursor up, down, left, right (as seen on the screen); 5 picks a piece of yours, then 5 on the
@ square to play it (5 on the same square drops it); + swaps sides (the C47 plays the side to move; black: the board
@ turns); 9 C47 vs C47 until the end (a key: you take the side to move); 3 two players (the C47 only checks the
@ moves and the end); 7 a new game of normal chess; ÷ a Chess960 game (its number, three digits, or ÷ again for a
@ random one; castling: the king onto its own rook); 1 the level 2 -> 1 -> 0
@ (CRAZY: random legal moves, a crazy face by the title);
@ 0 ends: the game is kept (CHN, GH, TW, TB) and goes on at the next start. The clock: 5 minutes each, 10 s more
@ for each move played, for the sides a person plays (the C47's clock stops while it thinks); a person whose time
@ runs out loses (LOST ON TIME), or draws when the opponent cannot mate (FIDE 6.9). The C47 thinks about 10-20 s at level 2 (the simulator's step count), 1-3 s at level 1.
@ The user's stack and LASTx are kept (LocR 9: STOS R.00, LASTx in R.08); R00-R19 are not touched; at the end
@ R20-R99 are cleared, the matrices BD GL GH OB deleted, WSIZE 64, your integer mode back (R.09), GRMOD 0 and GRFNT 20 set.
@ The integer mode is UNSIGN while CHESS47 runs: its words are bit patterns (keys to 2^32, glyph columns to 2^24, the
@ knights to 2^64): in 2COMPL SINT refuses the top bit (Out of range).
@ Registers: R20 the side to move (+1 white, -1 black), R21 the en passant square (0 none), R22 the castling word,
@ R23 SC, R24 R25 the white and black king's squares, R26 the level, R27 your side, R28 the cursor, R29 the
@ square picked (0 none); R30-R39 the move generator (R30 the square, R31 its piece x its colour, R32 the colour,
@ R33 the target, R34 the direction, R35 R36 the first and the end offset register, R37 the moves, R38 the row
@ of ML, R39 1 with castling); R40-R49 make / unmake (R40 the move, R41 from, R42 to, R43 the piece, R44 the
@ piece taken, R45 its colour, R46 the ply: the row of UN, R47 1 en passant / 2 castling / 3 Chess960's castling,
@ R48 R49 work);
@ R50-R56 attacked? (R50 the square, R51 the colour of the attackers, R52 the target, R53 the direction, R54 its
@ register, R55 the answer, R56 work); R57-R59 the castling word before the move of ply 1-3; R60-R71 the
@ search (R60 its side, R61 the best value, R62 the best move, R63 R64 the root move and count, R65 the value,
@ R66 R67 the reply and count, R68 R69 the pass (1 the captures, 0 the others), R70 the cut, R71 a reply's
@ value); R72 the quiet moves of a generation; R73-R79 the last 7 moves (texts, R79 the newest); R80-R87 the knight's offsets, R88-R95 the
@ directions (R88-R91 the diagonals, R92-R95 the lines); R96 the move number, R97 1 when the game is over, R98 the
@ key, R99 an AGRAPH word. Drawing reuses R30-R36 and R48 R49.
@ Screen 400 x 240, row 0 = bottom: the board at x 12-203, rows 20-211 (python/layout.py); the panel at x 212.
@ Control flow in the C47 STRUCT commands (IF / ELSE / ENDIF, DO / WHILE / ENDDO), indented two spaces per
@ level; tools/build_chess47.py checks them, numbers them and fills the REM ==== line from python/layout.py.
@

LBL "CHESS47"
LocR 10
STOS R.00
LASTx
STO R.08
@ your integer mode in R.09: at WSIZE 8, 1 SINT NOT ->REAL is 254 in UNSIGN, -2 in 2COMPL, -1 in 1COMPL, -126 in
@ SIGNMT (11111110 read in each mode); CHESS47 runs in UNSIGN and gives your mode back at the end
WSIZE 8
1
SINT
NOT
→REAL
STO R.09
XEQ 91                          ; -> :INIT:
@
@ ---- the game: your move (the keys) or the C47's, until 0 (R98 = 82) ----
DO 01
  RCL 20
  XEQ 74                        ; -> :HUMQ:
  X≠0?
  IF 01
    XEQ 56                      ; -> :HUMAN:
  ELSE 01
    XEQ 61                      ; -> :C47MV:
    RCL 27
    X=0?
    IF 02
      RCL 97
      X=0?
      IF 03
        0
        PAUSE 1
        KEY? 98
        1
        X=0?
        IF 04
          RCL 20
          XEQ 86                ; -> :SETME:
          XEQ 87                ; -> :STATE:
        ENDIF 04
      ENDIF 03
    ENDIF 02
  ENDIF 01
  82
  RCL 98
  X≠Y?
WHILE 01
ENDDO 01
@
